<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is a ball transfer table?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A ball transfer table is a flat surface embedded with omnidirectional ball casters that allow products to move in any horizontal direction with minimal push force. Each unit contains a large load ball on smaller support balls. Ball transfer tables are used at packing stations, conveyor intersections, and manual case handling areas in packaging lines.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">How much weight can a ball transfer table hold?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>It depends on the unit rating and how many units sit under the product. Alwayse groups units into light (0 to 100 kg), medium (101 to 249 kg), and heavy (250 kg and above) duty classes, with some heavy units rated in tonnes. Ratings vary by design, not just ball size: Omnitrack rates its MN25 spring-loaded unit with a 1-inch ball at 220 lb. Alwayse advises sizing so each unit can carry about one-third of the total load weight, because a load may rest on only three units.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">What is the difference between fixed and spring-loaded ball transfers?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Fixed ball transfers stay at a set height. Spring-loaded units have an internal spring that absorbs shock when loads are dropped onto the surface and helps share an uneven load across units. They do not retract out of the way for <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/">roller conveyors</a>. Pneumatic pop-up decks raise and lower on command for automated right-angle transfers.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">What ball transfer spacing pattern should I use?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Spacing depends on the smallest product footprint. Alwayse recommends dividing the shortest dimension of the load by 3.5, so a carton with a 12-inch short side would use roughly 3.4-inch centers. A staggered (diamond) pattern keeps more balls under a moving product than a square grid at the same spacing. The goal is at least three units under the product at every position.</p>

</div>
